/**
 * Standalone (no-collection) UVA problems, batch S (7 problems), under the 4-test-case standard
 * (Sample + boundary + tricky/adversarial case + stress test near the stated upper bound).
 *
 * - uva-11747-heavy-cycle-edges: an edge is the heaviest edge of some cycle if and only if Kruskal's
 *   algorithm REJECTS it (its endpoints are already connected by cheaper edges when it's considered,
 *   in increasing weight order) -- a standard MST cycle-property fact, made unambiguous here since the
 *   problem guarantees all weights are distinct (unique MST). Verified against the official sample (a
 *   very large real input). Boundary: 2 nodes, 1 edge (no cycle possible at all -- "forest"). Tricky: a
 *   3-node triangle (the classic minimal cycle), where the heaviest of the 3 edges is correctly
 *   identified and rejected by Kruskal's, hand-verified independent of the union-find bookkeeping.
 *   Stress: n=1000, m=25000 (both stated maximums) with deterministically-generated distinct edges and
 *   shuffled distinct weights (an earlier attempt using a simple LCG random generator with a
 *   collision-retry loop effectively hung after the generator's short cycle was exhausted around
 *   ~6000 distinct edges -- switched to deterministic generation, which trivially guarantees
 *   uniqueness by construction).
 * - uva-11764-cricket-field: a single left-to-right pass counting strictly-increasing steps (high
 *   jumps) and strictly-decreasing steps (low jumps); equal-height adjacent walls count as neither.
 *   Verified against the official sample. Boundary: a single wall (0 jumps possible at all, N-1=0).
 *   Tricky: 5 walls all the same height (0 high, 0 low, despite N=5>1 -- confirms equal-height steps
 *   are excluded from both counts, not silently added to one). Stress: T=29 (largest T<30) cases with
 *   N up to 49 (largest N<50) walls each.
 * - uva-11792-krochanska-is-here: BFS (unit-weight, since every line segment takes exactly 2 hours)
 *   from every "important" station (one used by 2+ distinct lines) to every other important station,
 *   picking the station with the minimum average travel time (ties broken by smallest station number).
 *   Verified against the official sample. Boundary: 2 lines sharing a single middle station (station 2
 *   is the only important station, trivially the answer). Tricky: 3 lines specifically constructed so
 *   that 3 DIFFERENT stations (2, 3, and 6) each become important, and are all mutually exactly 1 stop
 *   (2 hours) apart via direct edges from 3 different lines -- a genuine 3-way tie in average distance
 *   (each averages exactly 2 hours to the other two), requiring the tie-break rule to correctly select
 *   station 2 (not just whichever the algorithm happens to visit first in an unordered structure).
 *   Stress: N=10000, S=100 (both stated maximums).
 * - uva-11806-cheerleaders: inclusion-exclusion over the 4 sides (top/bottom/left/right row or column
 *   excluded from having ANY cheerleader), computing available-cell counts for each of the 16 subsets
 *   via row/column overlap arithmetic, then choosing K cheerleaders from the available cells with sign
 *   (-1)^|excluded sides|, summed mod 1000007. Binomial coefficients computed via a Pascal's-triangle
 *   DP (not modular inverse), since 1000007 is not guaranteed prime and the board is tiny (<=400
 *   cells) anyway. Verified against the official sample. Boundary: a 2x2 board with K=4 (every cell
 *   must be filled, trivially the unique way, and trivially covers all 4 sides since it's the whole
 *   board). Tricky: a 2x2 board with K=0 (impossible to have a cheerleader on every side with zero
 *   cheerleaders -- 0 ways, confirming the inclusion-exclusion correctly zeroes out rather than
 *   returning some spurious positive count). Stress: M=N=20, K=500 (all three stated maximums).
 * - uva-11824-a-minimum-land-price: since a land's price is 2*L^t (exponential in the YEAR t it's
 *   purchased, not the cost), the standard exchange-argument greedy applies: sort lands by cost
 *   DESCENDING and buy the most expensive one first (smallest exponent), since a larger base suffers
 *   far more from a larger exponent than a smaller base does. Verified against the official sample,
 *   including reproducing the problem statement's own illustrative example (costs 7,2,10, though the
 *   statement's own example uses a non-optimal order just to demonstrate the pricing FORMULA) as the
 *   real sample's actual case 1, confirming the optimal descending-order total is 134. Boundary: a
 *   single land (trivially bought in year 1, price 2*L). Tricky: reuses the exact 7,2,10 example as a
 *   permanent regression test. Stress: 39 lands (just under the stated "fewer than 40" bound) with
 *   large costs guaranteed to exceed the budget ("Too expensive").
 * - uva-11908-skyscraper: classic weighted interval scheduling -- sort advertisements by end floor,
 *   DP with binary search for the latest compatible (non-overlapping) previous advertisement. Verified
 *   against the official sample (a very large real input). Boundary: a single advertisement (trivially
 *   accepted, profit = its own value). Tricky: two FULLY overlapping advertisements with different
 *   values (50 and 100) -- only one can be placed at all, and the DP must correctly pick the
 *   higher-value one (100), not just the first-considered one. Stress: N=30000 (stated max)
 *   advertisements with random positions, sizes, and values.
 * - uva-11917-do-your-own-homework: direct lookup against the subject-days map, comparing against D
 *   and D+5 with correct boundary inclusivity (<=D is "Yesss", <=D+5 is "Late", otherwise or if the
 *   subject isn't in Sparrow's list at all is "Do your own homework!"). Verified against the official
 *   sample. Boundary: a subject needing exactly D days (the <=D boundary, "Yesss"). Tricky: a subject
 *   needing exactly D+5 days (the <=D+5 boundary -- still "Late", not "Do your own homework!", testing
 *   that the second comparison is also inclusive, not just the first). Stress: T=100 (stated max) cases
 *   with randomly generated subject lists and lookups.
 */
